      "text": "The problem presents a problem with finding the total number of possible combinations needed for a 3-numbered suitcase lock with digits between 0 and 9. The fact that the dial is of 3 numbers means that there are 10 options for each number. Therefore, the number of combinations of 3 different numbers can be calculated as follows:\n\nThe unit digit (one's place) has 10 options (0-9).\nThe ten-digit (the tens place) also has 10 options.\nThe hundred-digit (the hundreds place) also has 10 options.\n\nHence, the total number of combinations is calculated by multiplying the number of options for each place:\n\\[ \\text{Total combinations} = 10 \\times 10 \\times 10 = 10^3 = 1000. \\]\n\nThus, the suitcase lock has a total of $\\boxed{1000}$ different settings.",
